          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Leo Tolstoy</strong><br>\nLeo Tolstoy was born in 1828 to a noble family in Russia. At a young age, he lost his parents and was raised by relatives. Despite this tragedy, Tolstoy received an excellent education at home and later studied languages and law at Kazan University. However, he never completed his degree, leaving university in 1847 due to his dissatisfaction with the formal education system.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tolstoy's Military Service</strong><br>\nAfter leaving university, Tolstoy returned to his family estate and soon entered the Russian army in 1851. He served during the Crimean War, an experience that deeply influenced his perspective on life and death. His military service provided him with material for his future writings, including the \"Sevastopol Sketches,\" which brought him critical acclaim.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tolstoy's Writing Career Begins</strong><br>\nTolstoy began his writing career in the 1850s after his experiences in the Crimean War. His early works were autobiographical, but they also touched on broader social and moral issues. This early period produced works like \"Childhood,\" \"Boyhood,\" and \"Youth,\" which were part of a larger project that Tolstoy would never complete.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
