          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tolstoy's \"War and Peace\"</strong><br>\nWar and Peace, completed in 1869, is considered one of Tolstoy's masterpieces and a significant work in world literature. The novel, set during the Napoleonic Wars, follows multiple characters and explores themes of war, peace, and human existence. Tolstoy blended historical facts with fiction, creating a vivid portrayal of Russian society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Anna Karenina\" and Realism</strong><br>\nWith \"Anna Karenina,\" which Tolstoy finished in 1877, he continued to use realist techniques to explore themes of love, family, politics, and religion. The novel is often praised for its complex characters and depth of emotion, especially in its portrayal of Anna's tragic life and moral dilemmas.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tolstoy's Religious and Philosophical Beliefs</strong><br>\nIn the late 1870s, Tolstoy underwent a moral and spiritual crisis, leading to his renunciation of wealth and the adoption of a simplistic lifestyle. He developed unique religious and ethical beliefs based on non-violence, poverty, and chastity, which he expounded in writings such as \"A Confession\" and \"The Kingdom of God Is Within You.\"</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
