          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tolstoy's Influence on Nonviolent Resistance</strong><br>\nTolstoy's ideas on nonviolent resistance had a significant impact on global figures like Mahatma Gandhi and Martin Luther King Jr. He corresponded with Gandhi and his concept of \"passive resistance\" was influenced by Tolstoy's advocacy for nonviolence. His philosophical works continue to be a source of inspiration for leaders and movements around the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tolstoy's Educational Reforms</strong><br>\nTolstoy was also passionate about education reform. He established a school for peasant children on his estate, emphasizing practical skills and moral education over rote memorization. He believed education should be tailored to the needs and culture of the students, a revolutionary idea at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Legacy of Leo Tolstoy</strong><br>\nLeo Tolstoy died in 1910 at the age of 82, but his legacy as a writer, thinker, and social reformer lives on. His novels \"War and Peace\" and \"Anna Karenina\" are still widely read and studied for their artistic and philosophical depth. His beliefs in nonviolence and reform have left an indelible mark on human thought and history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
